<h1>Pallet Conveyors: CDLR, Chain &amp; Heavy-Duty Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Pallet conveyors are heavy-duty systems designed to transport loaded pallets, with rated capacity set by roller size, roller centers, chain, and frame. They integrate with <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">palletizers</a>, stretch wrappers, automated storage/retrieval systems (AS/RS), and dock operations. The most common types are <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR (Chain-Driven Live Roller)</a> and drag chain conveyors, with transfers and turntables for direction changes.</p>
<h2 id="subtypes-heading">Explore Pallet Conveyor Types</h2>
<ul class="usa-collection">{palletTypes.map((type) => (
<li class="usa-collection__item"><div class="usa-collection__body"><h3 class="usa-collection__heading"><a class="usa-link" href={type.slug}>{type.name}</a></h3><p class="usa-collection__description">{type.desc}</p>{type.tags && <p class="tags">{type.tags.map((tag: string) => <span class="usa-tag">{tag}</span>)}</p>}</div></li>
))}</ul>
<h2 id="cdlr-heading">Chain-Driven Live Roller (CDLR)</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> are the standard choice for pallet handling in packaging and distribution. Roller chain loops drive heavy steel rollers, usually roller-to-roller. Alba Manufacturing, for example, offers RC-40, RC-50, and RC-60 roller-to-roller chain on its CDLR (<a href="https://www.albamfg.com/products/chain-driven-live-roller-conveyor/chain-driven-live-roller-conveyor-2-9-16-diameter-rollers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alba CDLR</a>). The pallet rides on the roller surfaces, which are driven at a constant speed by the chain.</p>
<div class="photo-row"><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pallet-titan-525-cdlr.webp" width="720" height="517" alt="Short, wide roller conveyor section with closely spaced steel rollers, yellow guards along both sides, a motor and control box under the bed, and braced steel legs." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A Titan chain-driven live roller conveyor, the type used for heavy unit loads such as pallets. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.titanconveyors.com/products/chain-driven-live-roller/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Titan Conveyors</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/pallet-arrow-cdlr.webp" width="1000" height="833" alt="Rendering of a low, wide roller conveyor section with steel rollers, a yellow guard along one side, a red motor, and short floor supports." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A chain-driven live roller pallet conveyor from Arrow Conveyor, shown as a product rendering. A guard runs along one side of the bed. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.arrowconveyor.com/product/chain-driven-live-roller-pallet-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Arrow Conveyor</a></span></figcaption></figure></div>
<h3>CDLR Specifications</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Load capacity:</strong> rated per roller or per pallet. Titan Conveyors rates its CDLR models from 250 lb per roller (1.9" rollers on 4" centers) to 2,500 lb per roller (3-1/2" Schedule 80 pipe rollers on 6" centers) (<a href="https://www.titanconveyors.com/products/chain-driven-live-roller/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Titan CDLR</a>). Alba rates some models up to 15,000 lb.</li>
<li><strong>Roller diameter:</strong> the Titan range above runs from 1.9" to 3-1/2". Heavier loads use larger, thicker-wall rollers.</li>
<li><strong>Roller centers:</strong> Alba offers 2.75" to 12" centers on its 2-9/16" roller model. <a href="https://www.ashlandconveyor.com/pages/roller-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ashland's roller conveyor guide</a> says a minimum of three rollers should be in contact with the load at all times, so check centers against the pallet's bottom-board layout.</li>
<li><strong>Speed:</strong> Alba offers 30, 45, and 60 fpm fixed speeds. Pallet lines usually run slower than case conveyors.</li>
<li><strong>Frame:</strong> Heavy structural steel channel or formed sections</li>
</ul>
<h3>CDLR Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>High load capacity with positive chain drive</li>
<li>Smooth, controlled pallet transport</li>
<li>Zone accumulation available with clutches</li>
<li>Handles GMA-style stringer pallets, Euro pallets, and custom sizes when bottom boards span the rollers</li>
<li>Integrates easily with transfers, lifts, and automation</li>
</ul>
<h3>CDLR Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Requires pallet bottom boards parallel to conveyor direction</li>
<li>Block pallets may not convey well (no bottom boards)</li>
<li>Chain maintenance required (lubrication, tensioning)</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="chain-heading">Drag Chain Pallet Conveyors</h2>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/chain-conveyors/">Drag chain conveyors</a> (also called strand chain or pallet chain conveyors) use two or three strands of heavy chain running in tracks. The pallet bottom rides directly on the chain strands. Alba lists 2-strand and 3-strand as standard, RC-60 or RC-80 chain, and capacity up to 6,000 lb on its drag chain conveyor (<a href="https://www.albamfg.com/products/drag-chain-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Alba drag chain</a>). The design handles products that do not convey well on rollers, such as skids, odd pallets, and loads with uneven bottoms.</p>
<h3>When to Use Drag Chain</h3>
<ul>
<li>Block pallets or plastic pallets without bottom boards</li>
<li>Non-standard pallet sizes or mixed pallet types</li>
<li>Loads with bottoms that would hang up or drop between rollers</li>
<li>Applications where pallets enter from the side (perpendicular)</li>
<li>Integration with pallet dispensers or pallet magazines</li>
</ul>
<h3>Drag Chain Considerations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Chain slides in its track under load, so track wear strips and lubrication need attention</li>
<li>Pallet bottom boards must span across the chain strands</li>
<li>Noisier operation</li>
<li>Higher chain wear and lubrication requirements</li>
</ul>
